problem:
A solid lies between planes perpendicular to the x-axis at x = -11 and x = 11. The cross-sections perpendicular to the x-axis between these planes are squares whose bases run from the semicircle {eq}y=-\sqrt{121-x^{2}}

{/eq}to the semicircle {eq}y=\sqrt{121-x^{2}}

{/eq}. Find the volume of the solid.

We consider the two sequences $\left(a_{n}\right)_{n \geq 0}$ and $\left(b_{n}\right)_{n \geq 0}$ of integers, defined by $a_{0}=b_{0}=2$ and $a_{1}=b_{1}=14$ and by

$$
\begin{aligned}
a_{n} & =14 a_{n-1}+a_{n-2} \\
b_{n} & =6 b_{n-1}-b_{n-2}
\end{aligned}
$$

for $n \geq 2$.

Determine whether there are infinitely many numbers that occur in both sequences.

Use Laplace transforms to find a nontrivial solution to

{eq}\displaystyle

t{x}'' + (4t - 4){x}' + 4x = 0, x(0) = 0.

{/eq}

Require that {eq}x(1) = e^{-4}

{/eq} to find any arbitrary constant in your solution.
